A typical workflow may look as follows.

1. Install dependencies:
> conda env create -f env.yml

2. Add video to src/.

3. Create dataset:
> python make_raw_dataset.py src/VIDEO.mp4 raw_datasets/DATASET

4. Use labelme (included in env.yaml) to label images in raw_datasets/DATASET/train and raw_datasets/DATASET/val:
> labelme

5. Create YOLO compatible labels:
> python make_labelled_data.py raw_datasets/DATASET datasets/DATASET

6. Create dataset manifest DATA.yaml with all desired datasets.

7. Download base models:
> ./make_base_models.sh

8. Train model:
> python train.py base_models/BASE_MODEL.pt DATA.yaml models/MODEL.pt

9. Run inference:
> python find_danio.py models/MODEL.pt src/VIDEO.mp4 results/OUTPUT.mp4
> for f in src/*; do python find_danio.py models/MODEL "$f" "results/$(basename "$f")"; done

==============================================================================

Note that training is very resource intensive and not advised to do locally. Additional scripts are included to support training and inference on a SLURM scheduled compute cluster.

1. Training:
(remote) > sbatch train.sh base_models/BASE_MODEL.pt DATA.yaml models/MODEL.pt

2. Upload video files for processing:
 (local) > rsync -avP src/ REMOTE/src/

3. Run inference:
(remote) > for f in src/*; do sbatch find_danio.sh models/MODEL "$f" "results/$(basename "$f")"; done

4. Receive processed video files:
 (local) > rsync -avP REMOTE/results/ results/
